course: world
lesson: 58
title: People and society — communities, work, money and rules
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A community is a group of people who live, learn or work together and help one another — a family, a class, a village, a city or a whole country.
explain: In a community people do different jobs: farmers grow food, doctors treat illness, teachers teach, builders build. Because each person does what they are good at and swaps the results, everyone gets more than they could make alone.
explain: Long ago people traded by barter — swapping a sack of grain for a pair of shoes. Money made trade easier, because everyone accepts it and it can be saved and counted. People earn money by working and spend it on what they need.
explain: Rules and laws help people live together safely and fairly. Laws are made by a country's lawmakers, and everyone must follow them; traffic lights are a law that stops crashes at crossroads.
explain: Every person has rights, such as the right to be safe, to learn and to be treated fairly, and duties, such as respecting others and following the rules. Kindness and fairness make a community a good place for everyone, not only for the strongest.
fact: A community is a group of people who live, learn or work together.
fact: People do different jobs and exchange what they make or the services they give.
fact: Bartering means swapping goods without using money.
fact: Money makes trading easier because everyone accepts it.
fact: People earn money by working and spend it on needs and wants.
fact: Needs are things we must have to live, like food, water and shelter; wants are things that are nice to have.
fact: Taxes are money people pay to pay for things everyone shares, such as roads, schools and hospitals.
fact: Laws are rules for a whole country that everyone must follow.
fact: Every child has the right to be safe, to learn and to be treated fairly.
fact: Rights come with duties, such as respecting other people's rights.
fact: Being fair means treating people by the same rules, not favouring friends.
exercise: What is a community? | A group of people who live, learn or work together | One person living alone | A single building | Only the people in your family
exercise: Why do people in a town do different jobs? | Each does what they are good at and they share the results | Everyone must do the same job | Jobs are chosen at random every day | Only one job is ever needed
exercise: What is bartering? | Swapping goods without using money | Buying goods in a shop with coins and notes | Saving your money in a bank for later on | Giving things away for free to friends
exercise: Why is money easier than bartering? | Everyone accepts it, and it can be saved and counted | Money can be eaten when hungry | Money never runs out | Money makes goods free
exercise: Which of these is a need rather than a want? | Clean drinking water | A new video game to play | A second bicycle | A box of sweets
exercise: What are taxes used for? | Paying for things everyone shares, such as roads and schools | Paying the wages of shop owners | Buying things only for those who paid most | Paying for the private homes of rich people
exercise: Who must follow a country's laws? | Everyone in the country | Only children | Only police officers | Only people who agree with them
exercise: Why do we have traffic laws? | To keep people safe on the roads | To make journeys longer on purpose | To help cars go as fast as possible | To give police officers something to do
exercise: Which of these is a right every child has? | The right to learn at school | The right to take other people's things | The right to break rules | The right to never be told no
exercise: Which of these is a duty? | Respecting other people | Getting whatever you want | Always being first in line | Never helping out at home
exercise: A teacher gives a prize to her favourite pupil instead of the one who won the race. Why is this unfair? | The same rule was not applied to everyone | The prize was too small | Races should have no winner | The teacher should have kept the prize for herself
exercise: How does a baker get the money to buy flour? | By selling the bread they bake | By borrowing flour and never paying | By printing money at home | By paying taxes to the miller
exercise: Why do people who live together need rules? | So everyone can live safely and fairly | So the strongest people always win every time | So nobody ever has to agree | So that nothing ever changes
exercise: What happens when people in a community are kind to each other? | They trust one another more | They need more laws to stop them | They have fewer friends than before | They stop working together at all
test: A fisherman swaps three fish for a farmer's basket of apples. What is this called? | Bartering | Taxing | Saving | Borrowing
test: A family has a little money left this month. Which should they buy first? | Food for the week | A new television | A holiday souvenir | A fancy hat
test: Why would a village with no doctors, only farmers, find life hard? | Nobody would have the skill to treat illness | They would have far too much medicine to use up | Farmers do not need food | Farmers can never get ill
test: Which is true about rights and duties? | Having rights means respecting others' rights too | Having rights means you never have any duties at all | Only adults have rights | Only grown-ups have duties
test: Two children share a cake. One cuts and the other picks first. Why is this fair? | The cutter has a reason to make the pieces equal | The cutter always gets to keep the bigger piece of cake | The picker must take the smaller piece | Cutting first is always an advantage
test: Why does a country collect taxes from people who earn money? | To pay for shared things like hospitals and roads | To make everyone in the country poorer on purpose | To buy sweets for schools only | To stop people from working
test: In a class game, one boy keeps changing the rules so that he always wins. What is wrong? | Rules must be the same for everyone to be fair | Games should not have rules at all | He is just better at the game | The other children should stop playing forever
test: A new boy arrives at school and does not speak the language well. What is the kind thing to do? | Help him and include him in games | Ignore him until he learns the language | Laugh at his mistakes so he learns faster | Tell him to go to another school instead
test: why do we pay tax anyway | To pay for things everyone shares, like roads and schools | To make the shops richer | Only to punish people | So people cannot buy toys
test: swapped my stickers for his cards, what's that called | Bartering | Taxing | Saving | Borrowing
test: my cousin says a new games console is a need. true? | No, it is a want; needs are food, water and shelter | Yes, everyone must have one to live | Yes, all toys are needs | No, a console is a tax
test: Max reckons being fair means letting your friends win. right? | No, it means treating everyone by the same rules | Yes, friends come first | Yes, if they are your best friends | No, it means doing whatever you like, whatever the rules
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice designated as bartering? | The exchange of goods without recourse to money | The purchase of goods by means of a bank card in a shop | The payment of taxes to the government | The borrowing of money from a bank
exam: Identify, from the items hereinafter enumerated, that which constitutes a need rather than a want. | Shelter | A games console | A box of sweets | A fashionable jacket
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which taxes are levied upon the citizens of a country. | To defray the cost of shared provisions such as roads, schools and hospitals | To enrich the private persons who collect them | To purchase personal possessions for each taxpayer | To punish those who have committed crimes
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the relation between rights and duties. | Rights are accompanied by duties, such as respecting the rights of others | Rights exempt their holder from every duty | Duties are owed only by persons without rights | Rights and duties are wholly unconnected
